{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CYdHUaYGJBwtSVn8oZLsCS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JpULwAD9ufFLf2b6uF6xgr","name":"ToolSearch","input":{"query":"select:mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","max_results":1},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":957,"cache_read_input_tokens":60749,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":957},"output_tokens":40,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c49e676-dfc6-48dd-9895-282b92c4e684","uuid":"2574e563-fce1-4a84-97bf-e8425fcfd15a"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"toolu_01JpULwAD9ufFLf2b6uF6xgr","content":[{"type":"tool_reference","tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c49e676-dfc6-48dd-9895-282b92c4e684","uuid":"42b52d6d-ad62-488b-a624-f45099a9f65d","timestamp":"2026-05-16T06:17:15.648Z","tool_use_result":{"matches":["mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report"],"query":"select:mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","total_deferred_tools":23}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LCzVEFSckwFzPutZE61ctU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VV4GqSFRG4a9AtSR9Guxpg","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report: \"Principled Symbolic Consensus Improves LLM Reasoning\"\n\n**dream_id:** 20260516T054054 | **review_mode:** full | **reviewers:** R1 (Methods+Experiments), R2 (Novelty+Related Work)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Paper Summary\n\nSICA (Symbolic Invariant Consensus Aggregation) augments Self-Consistency (SC) by extracting first-order logic (FOL) constraints from LLM reasoning traces and using weighted partial MAX-SAT (Z3) to resolve voting ties. On 804 questions (FOLIO + ProofWriter D5) with Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct (K=12, T=0.7), SICA achieves 71.3% vs SC's 67.5% (+3.8pp, p=0.023). The paper also introduces C0 (multi-candidate rate) as a zero-cost predictor of SICA effectiveness (r=0.94 across 4 model scales) and characterizes an \"operating regime\" where SICA helps instruction models but degenerates for reasoning models (QwQ-32B).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Roundtable Process\n\n### Step 1: Independent Reviews\n\n**R1 (Methods + Experiments): Score 3 / Confidence 4**\n**R2 (Novelty + Related Work): Score 3 / Confidence 4**\n\n### Step 2: Cross-Sharing\n\nR1's statistical and experimental concerns (effective sample size of ~87 ties, even-K only ablation, single model family) reinforced R2's novelty concerns (the method only activates on ties, making its scope even narrower than the already narrow domain coverage). R2's identification of missing baselines (Logic-LM, LINC, ORM/PRM) complemented R1's observation that no evidence exists for SICA overriding non-tie majorities.\n\n### Step 3: Convergence\n\nBoth reviewers converged unanimously on:\n- **Score: 3 (Weak Reject)** — no disagreement\n- SICA's contribution is narrower than presented: it is functionally a tiebreaker, not a general consensus override mechanism\n- Benchmark coverage is critically narrow (2 FOL-domain benchmarks, 1 model family)\n- C0 as a \"contribution\" is partly tautological\n- Missing important baselines and related work\n- The operating regime finding, while directionally interesting, is underpowered (8 data points) and partially restates known phenomena\n\nNo substantive disagreements between reviewers.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Consolidated Strengths\n\n**S1. Clean algorithmic formulation.** The MAX-SAT weighting via intra-trace constraint consistency (Eq. 1-2) is technically sound and clearly described. Using Z3 makes it reproducible.\n\n**S2. Honest negative results.** Including QwQ-32B's degeneration and the ProofWriter failure case study (§5.7) is commendable. The operating regime characterization, even if directionally obvious, is explicitly formalized.\n\n**S3. Tiebreak baseline comparison (Table 3).** Comparing SICA against random, alphabetical, and first-seen tiebreakers directly addresses the \"just randomize ties\" objection. The 75.9% tiebreak win rate vs 50% expected from random is a real signal.\n\n**S4. Transparent overhead reporting.** The 39% wall-time breakdown (Table 7) with extraction-dominant cost is honest and practically useful.\n\n**S5. C0 as a deployment heuristic.** While trivial in isolation, providing an explicit threshold (C0 > 0.21) with a practitioner workflow (compute C0 → decide whether to run SICA) has practical value.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Consolidated Weaknesses\n\n### Major\n\n**W1. SICA is a tiebreaker, not a consensus mechanism — title and framing overstate the contribution.** [R1, R2]\nAll accuracy gains come exclusively from tie resolution. SICA never overrides a majority vote. The paper frames this as \"symbolic consensus\" implying it can leverage constraint quality to override incorrect majorities, but no evidence supports this. The effective \"treatment\" sample is ~87 tied questions out of 804. The title \"Principled Symbolic Consensus Improves LLM Reasoning\" should more accurately be \"Principled Symbolic Tiebreaking for Self-Consistency.\"\n\n**W2. Extremely narrow benchmark and domain coverage.** [R1, R2]\nOnly two benchmarks (FOLIO, ProofWriter D5), both in the formal logic domain where FOL extraction is naturally suited. No math reasoning (GSM8K, MATH), no commonsense (ARC, StrategyQA, CommonsenseQA), no code generation — all domains where SC is commonly deployed. The method fundamentally requires FOL-amenable problems, making generalization claims unjustified. The paper should either demonstrate cross-domain applicability or explicitly position itself as a logic-reasoning-specific contribution.\n\n**W3. Critical missing baselines and related work.** [R2, confirmed R1]\n- **Learned verifiers (ORM/PRM):** Lightman et al. (2023), Cobbe et al. (2021) — the most natural competitor for \"going beyond majority voting,\" entirely absent from both related work and experiments.\n- **Direct neuro-symbolic competitors:** Logic-LM (Pan et al., 2023) is cited but never compared experimentally — this is the minimum baseline since it uses the same LLM→FOL→solver pipeline single-path. LINC (Olausson et al., 2023), SatLM (Ye et al., 2023) — both directly relevant, neither cited.\n- **SOTA on same benchmarks:** Aristotle (ACL 2025), DyLoS (Xu et al., EACL 2026) — without these comparisons, absolute performance cannot be contextualized.\n- **Alternative multi-path methods:** Multiagent debate (Du et al., 2023), Best-of-N with self-verification.\n\n**W4. Statistical concerns mask thin effect.** [R1]\nThe headline p=0.023 on 804 questions obscures that only 87 questions (10.8%) have ties where SICA differs from SC. The effective comparison sample is 87, not 804. ProofWriter alone is not significant (p=0.11). No multiple testing correction across the 9+ reported experiments and many p-values. Confidence intervals for the K-ablation and model-scaling experiments (60-question subsets) are not reported — on 60 questions, ±1 correct answer swings the percentage by ~1.7pp.\n\n**W5. Even-K-only ablation is suspicious.** [R1]\nThe K ablation tests {6, 8, 12, 16, 20} — all even. Odd K values (K=11, 13) would dramatically reduce exact ties, likely collapsing SICA's primary advantage. The absence of odd-K experiments, combined with the finding that SICA only helps on ties, raises a serious concern that the method's benefit is an artifact of even-K sampling design.\n\n### Moderate\n\n**W6. Incremental novelty — SICA = Logic-LM + Self-Consistency.** [R2]\nBoth components (LLM→FOL extraction, solver-based reasoning) are well-established. The novelty is running extraction K times and aggregating, which is a straightforward combination. No formal guarantees connect MAX-SAT optimality to answer correctness.\n\n**W7. C0 diagnostic is partly tautological.** [R1, R2]\nC0 measures answer diversity, which is mechanically linked to SICA's gain: if all traces agree (C0→0), SICA cannot differ from SC by construction. The \"prediction\" that high C0 leads to high gap is a mathematical consequence, not an empirical finding. The regression (r=0.94 on 8 data points, 5 of which are the same model at different K — not independent) is underpowered and overfits. Anyone deploying SC already observes C0 implicitly.\n\n**W8. FOL constraint extraction quality is unvalidated.** [R1, R2]\nNo human evaluation of semantic correctness. \"Successfully extracting JSON\" ≠ \"semantically correct FOL.\" The QwQ-32B results reveal that biased constraints can make SICA worse than SC (biased extraction → SICA selects wrong answer). The paper's own case study shows 1/8 cases with harmful systematic errors. What is the true semantic accuracy rate?\n\n**W9. Single model family.** [R1, R2]\nAll experiments use Qwen2.5 variants. No Llama, Mistral, GPT, or other architecture. The claim that SICA helps \"instruction models\" is validated on exactly one instruction model family.\n\n### Minor\n\n**W10.** Section 5.6 references \"Figure ??\" — a broken cross-reference in the manuscript.\n\n**W11.** The paper has no figures at all. For a paper claiming C0-gap correlation (r=0.94) and operating regime visualization, the absence of any scatter plots, bar charts, or diagrams is a significant presentation gap.\n\n**W12.** Wang et al. citation year should be verified (2022 submission / ICLR 2023).\n\n**W13.** \"First-order logic constraints\" is potentially misleading — these are LLM-generated pseudo-FOL with no correctness guarantees, not verified formal logic.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Questions for Authors\n\n1. Does SICA ever override a strict majority (e.g., 7:5 vote) and improve accuracy? If not, should the method be presented as a tiebreaker rather than a consensus mechanism?\n2. What happens with odd K (K=11, K=13)? How many ties remain, and does the advantage survive?\n3. How does SICA compare to Logic-LM (single-path FOL→solver) on FOLIO and ProofWriter? This is the minimum baseline for claiming multi-path aggregation adds value.\n4. What is the human-evaluated semantic accuracy of extracted FOL constraints?\n5. Can you provide results on at least one non-FOL benchmark (e.g., GSM8K with Qwen2.5-14B)?\n6. The C0 regression has 8 data points, 5 of which are the same model at different K. What are the confidence intervals on the correlation? Are these independent data points?\n7. What is the absolute SOTA on FOLIO and ProofWriter D5, and where does SICA sit relative to it?\n\n---\n\n## 6. Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nComparing against the experiment design (review_context.yaml):\n\n| Experiment | Status | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| exp-001: Full 804Q evaluation | ✅ Covered (Table 1) | Main result present |\n| exp-002: K ablation | ✅ Covered (Table 4) | Even-K only, no odd-K |\n| exp-003: Tiebreak baselines | ✅ Covered (Table 3) | Well-executed |\n| exp-004: 7B scaling | ✅ Covered (Table 5) | Only 60Q, no CIs |\n| exp-005: QwQ operating regime | ✅ Covered (Table 5) | Only 48Q, but valuable negative result |\n| exp-006: C0 diagnostic | ✅ Covered (Table 6) | 8 points, underpowered |\n| exp-007: 72B-AWQ | ✅ Covered (Table 5) | Only 60Q |\n| exp-008: Case studies | ✅ Covered (§5.7) | 8 cases, adequate |\n| exp-009: Overhead analysis | ✅ Covered (Table 7) | Transparent |\n\nAll 9 planned experiments are present in the paper. Coverage is complete but depth is limited by small sample sizes on exp-002/004/005/007 (60Q or fewer).\n\n**Missing from design:** No figures at all (the design calls for Figs 1-13). The broken \"Figure ??\" reference in §5.6 suggests figures were planned but not generated/included.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Scores\n\n| Criterion | R1 | R2 | Consensus |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Soundness | 3 | 3 | 3 |\n| Novelty | 3 | 2.5 | 2.5 |\n| Significance | 3 | 2.5 | 2.5 |\n| Presentation | 3 | 3.5 | 3 |\n| **Overall** | **3** | **3** | **3 (Weak Reject)** |\n\n**Confidence:** 4/5 (both reviewers)\n\n---\n\n## 8. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3 — Weak Reject**\n\nThe paper presents a technically sound but narrow contribution. The core idea of using MAX-SAT over extracted FOL constraints to break Self-Consistency voting ties is reasonable and produces a real signal (75.9% tiebreak win rate). The operating regime characterization with QwQ-32B is a useful negative result.\n\nHowever, the contribution falls short of a top venue bar for three reinforcing reasons:\n\n1. **Scope mismatch with claims.** The method is a tiebreaker that activates on ~10% of questions, but is framed as \"symbolic consensus\" that \"improves LLM reasoning.\" The +3.8pp gain, while statistically significant, comes entirely from resolving 87 tie cases.\n\n2. **Narrow evaluation.** Two FOL-domain benchmarks, one model family. No evidence of generalization to math, commonsense, or any non-logic domain. Critical baselines (Logic-LM, LINC, ORM/PRM) are missing.\n\n3. **Incremental novelty.** The method is a straightforward combination of Logic-LM-style extraction with Self-Consistency sampling. The C0 diagnostic and operating regime characterization are directionally interesting but underpowered and partly tautological.\n\n**What would move this toward acceptance:**\n- Add odd-K ablation to confirm gains survive when ties are rare\n- Compare against Logic-LM, LINC, and at least one learned verifier baseline\n- Include at least one non-logic benchmark (even if results are negative — honest scope characterization is valuable)\n- Validate on a second model family (Llama or Mistral)\n- Add the planned figures (the C0-gap scatter plot is the paper's strongest visual argument)\n- Reframe honestly as a tiebreaking method with a principled constraint-based mechanism, rather than a general consensus improvement","project":"symb_invariant_consensus","tier":"best","dream_id":"20260516T054054","overall_score":"weak reject","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":667,"cache_read_input_tokens":61706,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":667},"output_tokens":58,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c49e676-dfc6-48dd-9895-282b92c4e684","uuid":"25a53fa3-b359-42f4-9ecc-788aadcefeeb"}
